<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall</h1><p class="st-meta">1280 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Schwäbisch Hall</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Salt-producing imperial city on the Kocher that gave its name to the heller coin and became an early stronghold of the Reformation under Johannes Brenz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hall grew around salt springs on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocher">Kocher</a>, worked since Celtic times. Its mint produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heller-coin">Haller pfennig</a>, or heller, from the 12th century. The town was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> possession, and in 1280 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">King Rudolf I</a> confirmed its independence from local lords, establishing it as an imperial city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Patrician families who owned shares in the salt works dominated the council until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> gained seats in the early 16th century. Salt boiling provided most of the city's wealth. Hall built a sizeable rural territory, buying <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-limpurg">Limpurg</a> castle in 1541. From 1522 the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brenz">Johannes Brenz</a> led a moderate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> that influenced much of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city was annexed by Württemberg in 1802, and the salt works became a state enterprise. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, its old town and the great steps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-michaels-church-schwabisch-hall">St. Michael's Church</a> well preserved.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Roman Eisele · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schw%C3%A4bisch_Hall_-_Altstadt_-_Salinenstra%C3%9Fe_-_Ansicht_von_NNW_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1280 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schwäbisch Hall</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hall, Schwäbisch Hall</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 464 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5225 720L5229 694M5225 720L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
